WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.320
Film Courage: I’m hoping we can talk about
a character with a goal and a character with

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.880
no goal.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.120
And how will I be able to see that my screenplay
has a character with a weak goal?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.950
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: Interesting.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:19.980
I think most movies mainstream movies that
you watch are really built around a character

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.900
pursuing something right?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.680
I would say they all have pretty definable
goals.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.060
Any movie that you see that is in the theater…I
take that back…not any movie that you see…most

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.250
movies that you see are designed around somebody
who wants something very badly and goes after

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.770
it, right?

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:47.090
I would say if you’re trying to figure out
whether your character has a weak goal probably

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.430
the places that I would start is at your Break
in [Act] 2, this is assuming you have already

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.930
written the script.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:58.610
At your break into 2 do we know what your
character is going to be pursuing over the

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.700
rest of the movie.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.460
That is their goal.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.379
You might not always think of it as a goal.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:10.999
You might think of it as just a conflict like
this character and this character are competing

00:01:10.999 --> 00:01:12.930
for the same prize or something like that.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.310
You could look at it that way.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:18.539
But we know what we are tracking over that
entire movie.

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:23.219
So I would say establishing that goal is one
part of the question that you are asking.

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:29.039
And the other part is whether they are pursuing
that goal and we want them to be pursuing

00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:36.289
it consistently and against great odds and
opposition right throughout the entire movie.

00:01:36.289 --> 00:01:42.069
That is something that you’d have to evaluate,
is my character in virtually every scene are

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:47.450
they pursuing that goal, that big picture
thing that they are trying to accomplish.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:55.029
If they are, are they also running into some
form of antagonism that is keeping them from

00:01:55.029 --> 00:02:00.159
making progress too quickly or achieving that
goal right away without anything standing

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:03.419
in their way because we want both of those
things because we want a strong goal but we

00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:09.000
also want your character to pursue it actively
and really try to get it.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.230
Film Courage: With the movie BRIDESMAIDS if
we go back to that movie as an example and

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.980
we look at her goals [Annie played by Kristen
Wiig)] is the goal to win the friendship of

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.469
the friend [Lillian played by Maya Rudolph]
or show up the other woman [Helen played by

00:02:23.469 --> 00:02:24.469
Rose Byrne]?

00:02:24.469 --> 00:02:30.280
Naomi: Well I would say that they goal in
that movie is to be her best friends Maid

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.430
of Honor.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:38.750
And the method (remember we talked about the
method) that she is going to use is by competing

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.849
with Helen.

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.549
She won’t always have to compete with Helen
in order to achieve that goal but that’s

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:50.390
her main force of antagonism so she really
is going to have to compete with Helen most

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:51.920
of the time.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.069
I would say what is at stake in that movie
is her friendship among other things but we

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:02.430
are just sort of laying out the building blocks
of the set up.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.959
That’s how I would kind of delineate them.

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:05.959
Does that make sense?

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:06.959
Film Courage: It does.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:11.730
So if I am writing a script and someone is
not clear on why my character is doing certain

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.690
things, does that indicate that the hero’s
goal is not very clear?

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:20.170
Naomi: If we know what they are trying to
do then I would say the goal is clear but

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:26.659
if we are not sure why they are doing something
I would say either there is sort of a plot

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:31.200
logic problem like we don’t understand why
they think this thing is what they need to

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.900
do or it could also be a stakes problem.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:40.540
We don’t understand why they are taking
on this goal in the first place because we

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.579
don’t understand what it means to them or
what will happen if they don’t take it on

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:46.970
or what happens if they fail.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:52.069
All of those things we need to really understand
the motivation behind your character’s actions

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:54.010
and a lot of times that has to do with stakes.

